 1 big thing: Lasting changes 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 That wince you just made while researching travel options? Get used to it.

 Why it matters: Soaring jet fuel prices are accelerating changes across the airline industry, showing up in higher fares, premium-heavy cabins and fewer flight options for travelers.

 ✈️ State of play: All of the major airlines are feeling the pain:

 American Airlines reported today that its fuel bill jumped nearly 70% in the third quarter from a year ago, adding $1.9 billion in costs and undercutting its revenue growth. Southwest and United Airlines both slashed their profit forecasts this week on higher fuel costs. And Delta Air Lines expects its fuel bill this year to come in $6 billion above last year's. 💸 The impact: To protect their bottom lines, airlines are raising fares and turning to more premium seats to squeeze more revenue out of their flights.

 The big picture: Airline executives see these changes — many of which were already underway — as a necessary new normal, not just a temporary response to fuel prices.

 United CEO Scott Kirby told investors yesterday that the industry's underlying costs — including airport fees, maintenance and engines — have increased so much in recent years that airlines must fundamentally change their economics. 🏷️ Zoom in: Executives echoed Delta CEO Ed Bastian's comments on pricing from earlier in the month, suggesting this week that the higher fares and other changes to protect profitability are likely to remain in place even after jet fuel prices ease.

 And airlines are becoming increasingly reluctant to operate flights that don't make money — potentially leaving travelers with fewer options. United's Kirby predicted that trend will continue across the industry. "One golden rule: Don't fly places that lose money," he said on the company's earnings call. "There's going to be less loss-making flying," he predicted. The bottom line: Fuel prices may eventually come back down. Don't count on airfares following.

