 2. P&amp;G&#x27;s balancing act 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep ; Chart: Pete Gannon/Axios Procter &amp; Gamble shares ticked up today as the consumer products giant showed it could walk the tightrope between competitive pricing and profitability, Axios' Pete Gannon writes .

 The maker of Tide, Charmin and Pampers reported 2.4% volume growth in the September quarter, led by its fabric and home care products. Organic revenue rose 4% in the quarter, the fastest growth in three years, per Bloomberg . 🏷️ Zoom out: P&amp;G has been taking a selective approach to price increases in a bid to win over cost-conscious consumers — even while it faces increasing cost headwinds.

 That appears to be paying off, as the volume gains suggest. 🗣️ What we're watching: P&amp;G isn't ruling out price increases to offset soaring costs, but executives are wary of jeopardizing sales growth.

 It's a "very fragile balance that we want to make sure we don't compromise on," P&amp;G CFO Andre Schulten said on the earnings call today. "So we will price and we will price with innovation, not jeopardizing the top line momentum." 
 
