 4. David&#x27;s Bridal says &quot;AI do&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Maura Kearns/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Here comes the AI wedding tool, all dressed in the black .

 💍 State of play: David's Bridal plans to spin off Pearl, its already profitable AI-powered wedding planner, as a publicly traded company with an anticipated valuation of $350 million, CNBC reports .

 David's Bridal CEO Kelly Cook will become CEO of Pearl as part of the spinoff. 👰 How it works: "Pearl's services include an agentic AI-powered wedding-planning platform, a proprietary tool to streamline appointments and communications and a digital content network with original programming," CNBC notes.

 "It uses AI to personalize its suggestions based on its data collection for each client." 💭 Nathan's thought bubble: This is a surprisingly positive turn of events for David's Bridal after it filed for bankruptcy protection in 2018 and then again in 2023 . If couples can use this tool to cut down on notoriously exorbitant wedding costs, this could have significant potential.

 
 
